  "question": "How did Chinese sources describe the Roman empire?",
  "question_lang": null,
  "answer": "The Weilüe, a Chinese text composed in the early third century CE, describes the Roman Empire (Da Qin) as a vast kingdom located west of Parthia and the Great Sea, containing \"more than four hundred smaller cities and towns\" and extending \"several thousand li in all directions.\" [S1] According to this source, the Roman capital was situated \"close to the mouth of a river\" with \"outer walls of the city made of stone.\" [S1] A later Chinese historical text, the Hou Hanshu, records that in 166 CE the king of Da Qin sent envoys bearing elephant tusks, rhinoceros horn, and turtle shell, though the text notes that \"the tribute brought was neither precious nor rare, raising suspicion that the accounts [of the 'envoys'] might be exaggerated.\" [S3]",
